 { Georg Meier – Sebastien Feller, Lubbock 2011 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of how White may pressure the d6-pawn to force Black’s army into submission. 2. Please note White’s ideal piece coordination. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. d4 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Re1 c6 9. Bf1 exd4 10. Nxd4 { We reach the structure of interest. As mentioned earlier, this structure is often just inferior for Black. To provide some evidence, note that in this position White scores above 65% on GM-level games, which is well above the usual 53-54% White should obtain on average. } Re8 ( { An alternative was } 10... Ng4 $5 11. Qxg4 Bxd4 12. Qd1 Qf6 13. Be3 Bxe3 14. Rxe3 $14 { though White is more comfortable as the d6-pawn is vulnerable. } ) 
11. Bf4 $1 Nc5 12. Qc2 Ng4 ( { PH: Black cannot exploit the unprotected d4-knight with } 12... Nfxe4 13. Nxe4 Bxd4 { because of the very strong } 14. Bg5 $1 { . } f6 { is not possible due to } 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Nxf6 { and the rook is hanging in the end, while } Qd7 15. Nf6 Bxf6 16. Bxf6 { gives White excellent compensation, the dark-squared bishop is unopposed. } ) 
 { So far both players are following one of the main lines in this variation, and now Meier goes into a good but less popular sideline with: } 13. Qd2 ( { More common is } 13. Rad1 $5 { though after } Qf6 14. Bg3 h5 { Black seems to have reasonable counterplay. } ) 
Ne5 ( { In the past, Black had managed to make a draw with } 13... Be5 14. h3 Bxf4 15. Qxf4 Ne5 16. Rad1 Qb6 { , as in the game Zueger – Jacimovic, Pula 1997, but after } 17. Qg3 $5 { ⩲ followed by f2-f4, there is no reason for Black to be optimistic, since } Qxb2 $2 { is met by } 18. f4 Ned7 19. Nf5 $16 { followed by Nxd6 with a dominant position. } ) 
 ( { A more reasonable alternative was } 13... a5 14. Rad1 a4 15. h3 Ne5 16. Be3 Qa5 { as in Zueger – Vogt, Switzerland 1997, but after } 17. f4 Ned7 18. Qc2 Nf6 19. Bf2 a3 ( { or } 19... Nh5 20. g3 ) 
20. b3 Nh5 21. g3 $14 { Black’s counterplay seems to runs out of fuel and we are left with a position similar to the game. } ) 
14. Rad1 Qb6 15. h3 a5 16. Be3 { Preparing f2-f4 gaining some space. } Ned7 { The knight must return sooner or later. } ( { The alternative } 16... Qb4 { is met by } 17. Qc1 $1 { when the c4-pawn is immune. For example, } Nxc4 $2 ( { or } 17... a4 $2 18. Nc2 Qa5 19. Rxd6 $16 ) 
 ( { So necessary is } 17... Qb6 18. f4 Ned7 19. Nf3 $14 { which is similar to the game. } ) 
18. a3 Qxb2 19. Bxc4 $16 ) 
17. Qc2 a4 18. f4 Qb4 ( { Or } 18... Nf6 19. Bf2 Qc7 20. g4 $5 { ⩲ when White’s kingside expansion is threatening, while Black lacks targets. } ) 
19. Bf2 Nb6 20. a3 Qa5 { White has been forced to weaken his b3-square, but this is not a problem at all, as he is ready to take concrete action on the kingside with: } 21. g4 { Black’s pieces have abandoned the kingside, and this attack is logical and strong. } Bd7 $6 { Black finally connects his rooks, but this move cuts the only path for Black’s knights to return in defence of the king, thus making White’s attack even more powerful. } ( { A better defence was: } 21... Nbd7 22. Nf3 ( { now the attack with } 21... Bd7 21. f5 $6 { will not work well due to } Ne5 $1 { since a knight on e5 can comfortably protect the kingside } ) 
Qc7 23. Bd4 Bxd4 24. Nxd4 $14 { White has a preferable position because of the space advantage and the weakened dark squares around Black’s king. } ) 
22. f5 $1 { ± The logical continuation. Black’s position is already close to lost. White’s attacking task is very simple and Black does not have a satisfactory defence. } Rad8 ( { The best defence was } 22... Bc8 $1 { allowing a black knight to come back: } 23. Nf3 Nbd7 24. Rxd6 $16 { But White is simply a pawn up. } ) 
23. Bh4 Rc8 24. Bf2 Rcd8 25. Bh4 { Of course White is not aiming for a repetition; he just needs time to decide the best way to impose his advantage. } Rc8 26. Nf3 { A good piece rearrangement. This move exerts pressure on the d6-pawn, and prepares the knight for a kingside invasion with Ng5. } Nb3 { Indirectly protecting the d6-pawn. } ( { If } 26... Bf8 27. Bg3 { , attacking the d6-pawn, and after } Nb3 28. e5 { Black is simply lost } ( { but not } 27... Nb3 27. Bxd6 $4 Bxd6 29. Rxd6 Qc5 { winning the rook } ) 
d5 29. e6 $1 fxe6 30. fxg6 $18 ) 
27. Qf2 $1 { A strong move, threatening Rxd6 as well as Ng5 and fxg6, invading down the f-file. } ( { Again not } 27. Rxd6 $4 Qc5 { winning the rook. } ) 
Bf8 { Protecting the d6-pawn, and preparing 28...Qc5. } ( { Trading queens with } 27... Qc5 { fails due to: } 28. Qxc5 dxc5 ( { or } 28... Nxc5 29. Rxd6 $16 ) 
