 ( 2. Qd2 $1 h6 3. Rad1 ( { But not } 3. f4 $2 gxf4 4. gxf4 Qh4 $17 ) 
Be5 $2 ( 3. Rad1 Rad8 $5 4. Qf2 Bf6 5. Rg1 Kh8 { Black has a playable position. } ) 
4. f4 $1 gxf4 5. gxf4 Bf6 6. Nde2 $1 { When Black's position collapses. } ) 
Be5 $1 { As we will soon see in Arutinian Efimenko, this move is very helpful, both covering f4 and protecting d6. } 3. Bf2 $6 { Too passive; White errs on the defensive side now. } ( 3. Nde2 Ne6 { White remains unable to play f3-f4, and insisting with } 4. Qd2 { runs into } Nxg3 $1 5. Nxg3 h5 { (Threatening ...h4) } 6. Bxg5 Qxg5 7. Qxg5 Nxg5 8. h4 Kh8 $1 9. Ne2 Ne6 { followed by ...Rg8 with a balanced game. } ) 
 ( 3. Nce2 { Seems more promising as } Ne6 $2 { now loses to } 4. Nf5 ) 
Qf6 4. Nce2 a5 5. Qd2 Kh8 6. Rg1 Rg8 7. Rdf1 Rg6 8. Be3 Rag8 $15 { Black is a little better, though it is not obvious yet how to create concrete threats. } 
