 { David Arutinian – Zahar Efimenko, Dresden 2007 Learning objective: This game is an example of how Black may gain counterplay with the idea ...g6-g5-g4. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. h3 Na6 7. Bg5 c6 8. Bd3 e5 9. O-O exd4 10. Nxd4 { We have reached the position of interest and the reader should notice how White’s pieces are strangely arranged for the structure, say compared to the earlier example Meier – Feller. White needs to find the time to play Re1-Bf1-Qd2, after which he should be able to secure a small advantage. } Re8 11. Re1 h6 ( { Black could play: } 11... Qb6 12. Nb3 Nd7 13. Be3 Qc7 14. Bf1 $5 ( { This yields an interesting variation, but } 13... h6 13. Nd4 { secures a small advantage after: } Nac5 15. Bc2 a5 16. b3 $14 ) 
Nac5 15. Bd4 Be5 16. Qd2 { Preparing f2-f4. } Ne6 $1 { Inviting an unfavourable trade. } 17. Be3 ( { But not } 16... h6 16. Bxe5 $6 dxe5 { which is studied in the next chapter. } ) 
Ndc5 18. Nd4 Nxd4 19. Bxd4 Ne6 $1 { The same idea again. } 20. Be3 ( { Again not: } 19... h6 19. Bxe5 $6 dxe5 $15 ) 
Nc5 { The position is approximately balanced. } 21. Bd4 $5 ( { Or } 20... h6 20. Rad1 a5 ) 
Ne6 $11 ) 
12. Bf4 Nc5 ( { White obtains a comfortable position after: } 12... Nd7 13. Bf1 Qf6 14. Be3 Nac5 15. Qc2 $14 ) 
13. Bc2 Nh5 14. Be3 Ne6 $1 { Proposing an exchange is often a good idea. } 15. Nxe6 ( { If } 15. Qd2 Bxd4 $1 16. Bxd4 Nef4 { Black gains good counterplay. For example: } 17. Kh2 Qh4 18. Rh1 $1 { The only defence } ( { but not } 18. Ne2 $2 Nxg2 $1 { and suddenly Black wins after } 19. Kxg2 Bxh3 20. Kf3 ( { or } 20. Kg1 Qg4 ) 
Bg4 21. Ke3 Qh3 ) 
 { . } Nxg2 $5 19. Kxg2 Bxh3 20. Rxh3 Nf4 21. Qxf4 Qxf4 { With a complicated game. } ) 
 ( { On the other hand } 15. Nf3 { is met by: } Ng5 $1 16. Nxg5 ( { better is } 16. Bd3 $5 { heading to f1 with a safe position } ) 
hxg5 17. Qd2 Nf4 18. Bxf4 gxf4 19. Qxf4 Be5 20. Qf3 Be6 21. Bd3 Qg5 $13 { White must be careful in light of the threat of ...Kg7 and ...Rh8xh3 . } ) 
 ( { PH: Perhaps the best move was } 15. Nf5 $5 { . After } gxf5 16. exf5 { Black has the interesting resource } Nd4 { when White has to spot the move } 17. Be4 { , and now Black has to play } Nxf5 { to stay in the game. The position remains complicated. } ) 
Bxe6 16. Bb3 $6 { On principle this cannot be a good move. The bishop is useless on b3, and it is too far from the kingside, and so unable to participate in its defence. } ( { Far more logical is } 16. Bd3 Be5 17. Bf1 Nf4 18. Qd2 Qf6 19. Rad1 { when Black is doing fine, but at least White’s kingside is safe for the moment. } ) 
Be5 $1 { An important move, preventing White’s f2-f4 advance and creating chances for a kingside attack. Black is not worse. } 17. Qf3 $2 { The queen will become the subject of an attack. } ( { Better was } 17. Bxh6 Qh4 $1 { Threatening ...g6-g5 and ...Bxh3 , and the only defence is: } 18. Qd2 $1 ( { Instead } 18. Be3 $2 { loses to } Bxh3 $1 19. gxh3 Qxh3 { . For example: } 20. f4 ( { waiting with } 20. Rc1 { allows forced mate with } Bh2 21. Kh1 Bg3 22. Kg1 Nf4 $1 23. Bxf4 Qh2 24. Kf1 Qxf2 ) 
Qg3 21. Kf1 Bxc3 22. bxc3 Rxe4 $19 ) 
 { But now: } Bxh3 $1 { This yields a draw by force. } ( 18. Qf3 Nf4 $5 { =∞ could also be considered. } ) 
19. gxh3 Qxh3 20. f4 Bd4 $1 21. Qxd4 Qg3 22. Kf1 Qf3 $11 { With perpetual check, since } 23. Qf2 $4 { loses to } Ng3 24. Kg1 Qh1 { . } ) 
Qh4 18. Rad1 ( { Of course not } 18. Bxh6 $2 { as after } g5 $1 { the bishop is trapped and } 19. g3 { does not help in view of: } Nxg3 20. fxg3 Bd4 $1 21. Kf1 Qxh3 22. Qg2 Qxh6 $19 ) 
g5 $1 { ⩱ The move ...g6-g5 is a recurring idea for Black in this position. It can serve two purposes: 1. Preventing White from achieving the favourable f2-f4. 2. Preparing the ...g5-g4 break to gain counterplay. Obviously this is Black’s plan here, taking advantage of White’s awkwardly-placed queen. } 19. g4 ( { If } 19. Bd4 $2 g4 $1 20. Qe3 ( { but not } 20. hxg4 $2 Bxg4 $19 ) 
gxh3 21. Bxe5 dxe5 22. g3 Qg5 $17 { and Black is a clear pawn up. } ) 
 ( { Instead } 19. Qe2 { is met by: } Nf4 ( 19. g4 g4 { no longer works due to } 20. g3 $1 Qxh3 21. Qd2 $5 { followed by Bxh6: Black’s queen is awkwardly placed } ) 
20. Bxf4 gxf4 $1 { Black will use the g-file } ( { or } 20. g4 Qxf4 21. g3 Qf6 $15 { when White cannot expand with f2-f4 } ) 
 { . } 21. Qf3 Kh7 22. Kf1 Rg8 23. Ke2 Qf6 24. Rd3 Rg6 25. Rg1 Rag8 $17 { White’s position is extremely uncomfortable and fragile. } ) 
Nf4 20. Bxf4 gxf4 21. c5 $6 { A sign of desperation, giving up a pawn in order to trade Black’s powerful bishop on e6 for the otherwise useless bishop on b3. Unfortunately, White will be a pawn down for no compensation. } ( { It was better to attempt a defence with: } 21. Rd3 Kh7 22. Kf1 Rad8 23. Red1 ( { but not } 23. Ke2 $2 { due to } f5 $1 24. gxf5 Bxf5 $17 25. exf5 $4 Bxc3 26. Kd1 Rxe1 $19 ) 
Rg8 $5 { ⩱ Even so, White’s position is difficult to handle due to the latent ...h6-h5 break. } ) 
Bxb3 22. axb3 dxc5 $17 { The rest of the game bears no relevance to us, though Black exploited his advantage very easily. Final remarks 1. White’s biggest mistake was playing 16. Bb3, as this bishop was needed in the defence of the kingside. 2. Black’s post-opening sequence (moves 11-18) deserve to be praised. Note how Black keeps the threats coming, managing to trade one minor piece, prevent f2-f4 with 16...Be5, and then even create a kingside attack with 18...Qh4. 3. From a static perspective (ignoring tactics or the initiative) Black’s ...g6-g5-g4 break does not look good. By trading his g-pawn for White’s h-pawn Black weakens his structure significantly. The point here is that Black cannot afford to obtain a passive position and this plan is a great method for obtaining activity. If Black executes the ...g6-g5-g4 break well, then White will not have the time to take advantage of Black’s structural weaknesses. } 
 0-1

