 { The following game is an interesting example from my own experience. I had always had trouble with this structure and I spent the first few hours of the game just hoping to lose in an honourable way, yet somehow things worked out for me in the end. This game has many flaws, but I think we can learn much from the mistakes both players made. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O Na6 8. Be3 Ng4 9. Bg5 Qe8 10. Re1 c6 $6 { A questionable move. My theoretical knowledge was very limited, so I chose to play schematically, just hoping things would turn out OK. } ( { Theory recommends } 10... exd4 11. Nxd4 ( { or } 10... c6 10. Nd5 $5 ) 
Qe5 12. Nf3 Qc5 13. Bh4 { when Whites forces are somewhat uncoordinated, which compensates for the space advantage, so chances are almost balanced. } ) 
11. h3 h6 { This is a standard response. } 12. Bc1 ( { Another option was } 12. Bh4 Nf6 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Qd6 $14 { as in Smirnov Savitskiy, Moscow 2013. } ) 
 ( { It is a bad idea to give up the bishop with } 12. hxg4 $6 hxg5 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Nxg5 Qe7 $1 15. Nh3 ( { or } 15. Nf3 Bxg4 $15 ) 
Nc5 $15 { and Black will follow up with ...Ne6-d4 gaining the superior position. } ) 
Nf6 ( { I did not want to clarify the centre with } 12... exd4 { as } 13. Nxd4 Nf6 14. Bf1 $14 { is pleasant for White. } ) 
13. Bf1 { Blacks position is already very uncomfortable. } ( { An alternative was } 13. Be3 Qe7 14. Bf1 Re8 { where I was hoping to maintain a flexible centre until White decided to play } 15. d5 c5 { transposing into a Kings Indian Type II structure. The problem for Black is that White can just play } 15. Qd2 Kh7 16. Rad1 $5 { ± to continue with a2-a3, b2-b4 and only then clarify the centre. Black is in trouble as he cannot create effective counterplay. } ) 
Nh5 $6 { Hoping to keep the central tension forever, and heading toward f4. But this idea is unlikely to work. } ( { White was also better after } 13... Qe7 14. Be3 Re8 15. Qd2 Kh7 16. Rad1 $16 { as explained above. } ) 
 ( { At this point defining the centre with } 13... exd4 { does not work well either, since after } 14. Qxd4 $1 Qe6 15. Bf4 { the d6-pawn cannot be protected as } Rd8 $2 { loses to } 16. Qd2 $1 Kh7 17. e5 { . } ) 
14. g3 $6 { There is no need for this move. } ( { Stronger was } 14. Be3 { when the natural } Qe7 ( { or } 14. g3 Nf4 15. c5 $1 { ± } ) 
 { is met by the thematic: } 15. c5 $1 exd4 ( { if } 15. g3 dxc5 $6 16. dxe5 { White is winning, say after } Bxe5 17. Nxe5 Qxe5 18. Bxh6 Re8 19. Bxa6 bxa6 20. Qd2 ) 
16. cxd6 Qxd6 17. e5 Qe7 18. Qxd4 $16 { Blacks knight is misplaced on h5 . } ) 
Qe7 15. Be3 Nc7 16. Rc1 Re8 17. Qd2 Kh7 { Still hoping to keep the central tension forever, though after: } 18. b4 $1 { The positional threats, d4-d5 or c4-c5, became too strong and I had to release the centre. By now I felt my position was close to collapse. } exd4 19. Nxd4 ( { An option was } 19. Bxd4 $5 { as trading dark-squared bishops favours White significantly: } Nf6 20. e5 $1 dxe5 21. Bxe5 Bf5 22. c5 $5 { ⩲ Blacks position is very uncomfortable. } ) 
a5 $6 { Hoping to open the a-file. } ( { A healthier alternative was } 19... Ne6 { attempting to trade off some pieces to reduce the space problem: } 20. Nxe6 ( 19... a5 19. Nde2 $6 { is met by } Ng5 $1 { when Black takes the lead } ) 
Bxe6 21. Red1 Red8 22. g4 Nf6 23. f4 $14 { Black is in trouble that is similar to Meier Feller, but the game is far from over. } ) 
20. a3 $2 { A serious and very instructive mistake. The opening of the a-file will create plenty of counterplay for Black. } ( { Correct was } 20. b5 $1 { keeping the a-file closed and forcing the creation of more weaknesses: } Ne6 ( { or } 20. a3 c5 21. Nf3 $16 { and the d6-pawn is a serious weakness } ) 
21. Nxe6 Bxe6 22. Red1 Red8 23. Na4 $16 { With a dominating position. } ) 
axb4 21. axb4 Ra3 $5 { Blacks pieces can breathe again, and suddenly I felt I had some chances to survive. } 22. Bg2 ( { If } 22. Qb2 Ra8 { and Black is fine. } ) 
 ( { But not } 22. Ra1 $6 { due to: } Rxc3 $1 23. Qxc3 c5 $15 ) 
Ne6 { Of course I was not going to miss an opportunity to trade off minor pieces. } 23. Nxe6 ( { It would be desirable to keep pieces on the board with } 23. Nde2 { but after: } Ng5 $1 24. Kh2 Nf6 25. f3 Qd7 $5 { White cannot easily neutralize Blacks play as } 26. h4 $2 ( { correct is } 26. Ng1 ) 
 { runs into the nice tactical shot: } Nxf3 27. Bxf3 Qh3 28. Kg1 Ng4 29. Bxg4 Bxg4 30. Rf1 Rxc3 $1 { Note how Blacks rook on a3 suddenly plays a crucial role: } 31. Nxc3 Qxg3 32. Kh1 Bf3 33. Rxf3 Qxf3 34. Kg1 f5 $1 { ∓ } ) 
Bxe6 { Chances are level, as Black has obtained reasonable counterplay. Something to know about this structure is that once Black manages to free his position, he will not fall into a passive position again. } 24. g4 Nf6 25. Bf4 Rd8 26. Nd5 { This looks strong, but it is nothing special due to: } Qf8 ( { Of course not: } 26... cxd5 $2 27. exd5 $16 ) 
27. Ne3 { Keeping knights on the board, but this will not help White much. } ( { If } 27. Nxf6 Bxf6 28. Red1 Be7 { and the position is equal. } ) 
Ne8 { Protecting the d6-pawn, covering the a1-square, and preparing to free the rook on d8 to invade down the a-file. } ( { A worthy alternative was } 27... h5 $5 28. g5 Ne8 29. Red1 Rda8 { . This is similar to the game but with the inclusion of ...h6-h5 and ...g5-g4 . } ) 
28. Red1 Rda8 { It is now White who must play for equality. } 29. Bg3 ( { Taking the pawn with } 29. Bxd6 $6 { is met by: } Qxd6 30. Qxd6 Nxd6 31. Rxd6 Bf8 32. Rdd1 Bxb4 $15 ) 
Ra2 30. Qe1 g5 $5 { A double-edged decision, intended to prevent f2-f4. After studying the previous two examples, this move seems natural, though it was not an easy decision for me back then. Blacks strategy consists of blockading the dark squares with ...Be5. } ( { A more solid option was: } 30... Qh8 $5 31. Rd3 ( { the expansion } 30... g5 30. f4 $6 { is met by } Bb2 $1 32. Rb1 Bd4 33. Bf2 Bxc4 $19 ) 
R8a3 32. Rxa3 Rxa3 33. b5 Ra2 $15 { When Blacks position is preferable. } ) 
31. c5 { A logical decision, undermining Blacks control of e5, thus preventing ...Be5. } dxc5 32. Rxc5 Nf6 { Planning ...Nd7 disputing the control of e5. } 33. Nf5 Nd7 34. Bd6 ( { If } 34. Rcc1 { then } Be5 $1 { ⩱ gaining control of the dark squares, and keeping the bishop on g2 restricted. } ) 
Qh8 35. e5 $5 { A very interesting resource, played just as we approached the time control. White sacrifices an exchange in order to obtain a dangerous attack against Blacks king. Fortunately for me, tactics were my forte, and I was able to respond. The rest of the game is unrelated to the topic of this chapter, but it is fun to watch, therefore I will leave it with some comments. } ( { If } 35. Rcc1 Be5 $5 { ⩱ and Black achieves his goal of controlling the dark squares. Whites position would become very uncomfortable. } ) 
 ( { Correct was } 35. Nxg7 Qxg7 36. Rcc1 Ne5 37. Rc5 Nd7 38. Rcc1 { and the game could have ended in a draw, or I could choose } h5 $5 { to continue the fight. } ) 
Nxc5 36. bxc5 Qe8 37. h4 R8a4 38. Be4 Bd5 $1 { An essential move. Suddenly Whites king is also at risk. } 39. Nd4+ ( { If } 39. Bb1 { then } Rxg4 40. Ng3 Kg8 41. Bxa2 Bxa2 $17 { and Whites kingside attack is over. } ) 
Kg8 40. hxg5 hxg5 41. Bb1 Qc8 $1 { A strong intermediate move. } 42. Bf5 ( { Of course not: } 42. Bxa2 $4 Qxg4 43. Kf1 Bg2 44. Kg1 Bf3 45. Kh2 Qg2 ) 
Qa8 $19 { Black has gained the time to reach a8 with the queen, and now the position is clearly winning for me. } 43. Bb1 Ra1 44. Qe3 Rxd4 $5 ( { Simpler was: } 44... Rb4 $1 45. Qd3 Rbxb1 46. Rxb1 Qa2 $1 47. Rxa1 Qxa1 48. Qf1 ( 47... Rxd4 47. Kh2 $2 Qh1 49. Kg3 Qg2 ) 
Qxd4 $19 ) 
45. Qxd4 Ra4 46. Qd3 Rxg4+ 47. Kh2 Rg2+ 48. Kh1 Rg4+ 49. Kh2 Rh4+ 50. Kg3 Qc8 ( { Neater was: } 50... Qa4 $1 51. Qf5 Rh3 52. Qxh3 ( 51... Qc8 51. Kxh3 Qh4 ) 
Qf4 ) 
51. Qf5 ( { No better is: } 51. e6 Qxe6 52. Qf5 Rh3 53. Qxh3 ( 53. Qxh3 Qh6 { 54.Kg3 Qh4# } ) 
Be5 54. f4 Bxf4 55. Bxf4 gxf4 56. Kh2 Qe2 $19 ) 
Be6 52. Qc2 Rh3+ 53. Kg2 Bd5+ ( { White resigns. Mate is unavoidable after: } 53... Bd5+ 53. Kf1 Qg4 55. Ke1 Qb4 $1 { For example: } 56. Kf1 Bg2 $1 57. Kxg2 ( { Or } 56... Bd5+ 56. Kg1 Bf3 ) 
Qg4 58. Kf1 Rh1 { Final remarks 1. Black was unable to maintain the central tension and was eventually forced to play ...exd4 obtaining an open King’s Indian. It would have been better to accept this pawn structure with 10...exd4 and begin searching for counterplay before White could consolidate his position. 2. White’s biggest mistake was 20. a3, allowing the a-file to be opened. From this point on, Black’s play was a good model of how Black may obtain counterplay. 3. The advance ...g6-g5 (as I chose on move 30) is not quite as uncommon as one might expect for such a wild-looking move. We have already seen some examples earlier in the chapter. } ) 

